# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# PATIENT DATA — human-subjects material. Never commit.
# No leading slash => these match at ANY depth, not just the repo
# root. That is deliberate: the old rule was `config/Centralized-*`,
# which silently failed the moment the file sat somewhere else.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
data/
Centralized-NIDAQ-System*
*_eventonly.csv

# The synthetic demo recordings are excluded too, by `data/` above. They contain no
# real data, but the tool is meant to be pointed at a researcher's own recordings
# rather than at a canned example. Regenerate them any time with:
#     python main.py demo

# Roster: participant IDs x session dates x clinical notes (sheet "draft").
docs/Pharma-rundown.xlsx

# The column layout, but filled with a real subject's measurements: 728 rows
# carrying a participant code, a session date and its EMG values. The authoritative
# layout is SCHEMA in src/harmonize.py, which does ship.
docs/Table-layout.csv

# Self-check output. Names session folders, which are subject codes plus dates.
# Use `selfcheck.py --anonymise` for a version that is safe to share outside the lab.
report.txt

# Process-flow workbook. The DATA-PROCESSING-FLOW sheet quotes a real filename —
# a subject code together with the exact minute of their session.
docs/data_processing_flow_06012026.xlsx

# Editor and assistant working directories, not part of the project.
.claude/
.agents/
.vscode/

# Reference papers. Journal PDFs cannot be redistributed, so they stay local; the
# method itself is written up in docs/cusum-method.md, which does ship.
docs/*.pdf

# Notebook outputs embed a real subject's EMG traces and P2P/AUC values,
# and subject IDs appear in the filenames. Ignoring the notebooks wholesale.
# NOTE: this means notebook code is NOT version-controlled. If you later want
# the code in git without the data, drop this rule and use nbstripout instead.
notebooks/*.ipynb

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# INTERNAL — working documents, not for the published repository.
#
# The repository carries what someone needs to understand and run the tool.
# These are lab-internal: findings about our own data, notes on what to do
# next, and material belonging to the lab rather than to this project.
# All still on disk; only tracking changes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
docs/security-review.md
docs/data-needed.md
docs/next-steps.md
docs/publishing.md
docs/WHY-WE-DO-WHAT-WE-DO.docx

# Inherited MATLAB. Kept locally as the reference this pipeline was checked
# against, but its provenance and licence are not ours to republish. The one
# thing that matters from it, the formula, is reimplemented and cited in
# src/compare_legacy.py.
docs/legacy/

# A scratch script for poking at one recording. Hardcodes channel numbers that
# the pipeline looks up properly, so it is a bad pattern to hand anyone.
explore.py

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# TESTS — kept out of the repository for now, not deleted.
#
# Delete this whole block to publish them again. That is worth doing when the
# repository goes to other labs: the suite is what makes claims like "peak-to-peak
# agrees exactly with the old MATLAB" checkable by someone else rather than taken
# on trust. While it is excluded, the CI test job skips itself and says so.
#
# The files are still on disk and `python -m pytest` still runs them locally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
src/tests/
pytest.ini

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment / build
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
.pytest_cache/
*.egg-info/


# Notebooks

.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Outputs / derived results
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
outputs/
results/

